A||N||1||LemonSky||What's an EGR valve?||||5||
Z||000000||LemonSky||07-21-2000||09:26 PM||||Pardon me for my ignorance, but I got the infamous "check engine" signal while driving my 1986 SE6. I did the computer check and got a "32" which means the EGR system needs checking. I looked in the owner's manuel (not very explanatory in general, BTW) and found nothing pertaining to an EGR valve. Please help. Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||07-22-2000||12:22 AM||||EGR is Exhaust Gas Recirculation. By allowing some exhaust to go backing to the intake the oxides of nitrogen (NOX) are reduced. Less smog.<P>The EGR is controlled by vacuum through a solenoid (EGR control solenoid). THE ECM checks that the EGR is working by a switch that looks for vacuum at the inlet to the solenoid. It only looks when it knows there should be vacuum (deceleration or idle). The vacuum will close the switch. The switch goes back to the ECM. If it doesn't detect this closed switch (it grounds the wire), it will give the code. <P>The problem could be a bad solenoid/switch assembly (not unusual) or a vaccum leak.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
